From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zed-patches <zed-patches@localhost>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 20:47:06 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] agent_ui: the lock's small modal opens under the lock;
 turning the jail on asks and restarts

Operator, 2026-11-25, two changes to 0174's small modal, the one the open lock
of an agent started outside the jail shows:

1. It opens right under the lock. It was a workspace modal, centred in the
   window, far from what was clicked. It is now a PopoverMenu whose trigger
   is the lock (attached at the lock's bottom-left corner), its handle kept
   by the ThreadView; the modal entity itself is unchanged.

2. Turning the switch on asks once, "Turn on the agent sandbox for <name>
   and restart the agent?" (Cancel / Turn On and Restart, Cancel first as
   every sandbox prompt), and on the confirming answer saves the setting and
   restarts the agent inside the jail. Before, it saved and left an amber
   lock for a second click. Turning off keeps the page's path (it asks
   first and restarts nothing).

The restart waits for the saved setting to come back through the settings
file: restarting at once would start the agent with the store still saying
"off", outside the jail. The wait runs on the window, not on the popover,
because the confirmation takes focus and the popover may be gone when the
setting lands; it watches SettingsStore and gives up, with a warning, after
ten seconds. The restart itself goes straight to restart_for_jail: the
confirmation already said a running turn stops, so restart_agent_for_jail's
own "Restart the agent?" would be a second question.

One click reaches the switch's handler up to three times -- SwitchField's row,
its Switch and, with a tab index, the Switch's inner element all carry it
(upstream ui) -- so the modal ignores the switch while a confirmation it
opened is pending. Without that, three prompts opened and each replaced the
one before (seen on Xvfb: three quick_modal_toggle lines, two cancelled);
0174's turn-off had the same three prompts.

New catalog entries (en-US, pt-BR): agent-jail-confirm-turn-on-project,
agent-jail-confirm-turn-on-detail, agent-jail-button-turn-on-restart; the
modal's hint now says what turning on does.

Tests (agent_jail_quick_modal_tests.rs): the popover starts under the lock
and lines up with it; turning on asks, and Cancel saves nothing and
restarts nothing; Turn On and Restart saves the project as on and restarts
the thread once the store reads it, with no second prompt. The test
settings store reloads the saved file by itself, so "no restart before the
setting lands" is held by the code's wait, not by a test.

In the ai-jail group, at its end.
---

diff --git a/crates/agent_sandbox/src/confirm.rs b/crates/agent_sandbox/src/confirm.rs
index ba0915b..c12d22a 100644
--- a/crates/agent_sandbox/src/confirm.rs
+++ b/crates/agent_sandbox/src/confirm.rs
@@ -77,6 +77,23 @@ pub fn confirm_turn_off(scope: OffScope, window: &mut Window, cx: &mut App) -> T
     )
 }
 
+/// Asks "Turn on the agent sandbox for <name> and restart the agent?" -- the lock's small modal
+/// (operator, 2026-11-25): turning the jail on there applies it at once instead of leaving an
+/// amber lock to click. `true` only for Turn On and Restart; Cancel comes first, as in
+/// [`turn_off_buttons`]. The page's switch still saves without asking (R4.1).
+pub fn confirm_turn_on_and_restart(name: &str, window: &mut Window, cx: &mut App) -> Task<bool> {
+    let message = t!("agent-jail-confirm-turn-on-project", name = name);
+    let cancel = t!("agent-jail-button-cancel");
+    let turn_on = t!("agent-jail-button-turn-on-restart");
+    ask(
+        &message,
+        &t!("agent-jail-confirm-turn-on-detail"),
+        &[cancel.as_str(), turn_on.as_str()],
+        window,
+        cx,
+    )
+}
+
 /// Asks "Forget <path>?" (R6.4). `true` only for Forget.
 pub fn confirm_forget(path: &str, window: &mut Window, cx: &mut App) -> Task<bool> {
     let message = t!("agent-jail-confirm-forget", path = path);
